702>>>>>>>>>>>// constants for RGB color schemes NOTE: these are for backwards compatibility - do not use them!
702>>>>>>>>>>>
702>>>>>>>>>>>// The following symbols mirror the "System-Colors" of Windows and
702>>>>>>>>>>>// can be used wherever a RGB value is expected
702>>>>>>>>>>>
702>>>>>>>>>>>// In VDF a rgb system color is recognized by setting the hi bit
702>>>>>>>>>>>// to true and passing the windows system color constant in the lower
702>>>>>>>>>>>// bytes. for example: ColorButtonFace in WinUser is $0F. In VDF you can
702>>>>>>>>>>>// set the color by setting the value $8000000F.
702>>>>>>>>>>>//     Set Color to $8000000F   or Set Color to clBtnFace
702>>>>>>>>>>>// VDF will do the system color conversion. It will also recognize that
702>>>>>>>>>>>// this is a system color and change the color if the system colors change.
702>>>>>>>>>>>//
702>>>>>>>>>>>//  If you were using any of these in the past you will now get different
702>>>>>>>>>>>//  values. You should be able to use them as before, but the actual value
702>>>>>>>>>>>//  is different. If you want to get the actual rgb color for this, use the
702>>>>>>>>>>>//  following: Move (GetSysColor(clName IAND $FFFFFF))
702>>>>>>>>>>>//  Note that these are better values for setting colors because they
702>>>>>>>>>>>//  will change if the system colors are changed.
702>>>>>>>>>>>Define clScrollBar           for (|CI$80000000)
702>>>>>>>>>>>Define clBackground          for (|CI$80000001)
702>>>>>>>>>>>Define clActiveCaption       for (|CI$80000002)
702>>>>>>>>>>>Define clInactiveCaption     for (|CI$80000003)
